# rmtfs resolves the modem's storage requests under /dev/disk/by-partlabel using
# the bare partition names. On this device modemst1, modemst2 and fsc are
# unsuffixed but fsg is A/B suffixed (fsg_a / fsg_b), so without an alias rmtfs
# would fail to serve the factory NV backup if the modem ever asked for it.
#
# The modem has not been observed requesting it (a cold boot only shows
# /boot/modem_fs1 and /boot/modem_fs2 being opened), so this is preventive.
#
# VERIFIED 2026-09-06 on the live device: the symlink is created and resolves.
#   /dev/disk/by-partlabel/fsg   -> ../../sdd13   (134217728 bytes)
#   /dev/disk/by-partlabel/fsg_a -> ../../sdd13
#   /dev/disk/by-partlabel/fsg_b -> ../../sdf13
# and `udevadm info -n /dev/sdd13` lists by-partlabel/fsg among its DEVLINKS.
# Confirmed by inspection of rmtfs's open fds that only modemst1 (sde12) and
# modemst2 (sde13) are actually held open; fsc (sde14) and fsg are not.
#
# CAVEAT, deliberately not fixed here: this hardcodes the _a slot. This phone
# boots slot _a (androidboot.slot_suffix=_a in /proc/cmdline) so the alias is
# correct today, but on a _b boot it would point the modem at the other slot's
# factory NV. udev cannot read the boot slot without a helper program, and
# rmtfs already has the right mechanism for this -- `-S b` sets the partlabel
# slot suffix (rmtfs.c, case 'S'). If this port ever boots _b, pass -S in the
# drop-in rather than making this rule cleverer.
ENV{PARTNAME}=="fsg_a", SYMLINK+="disk/by-partlabel/fsg"
